A||||4||Cooter||3.4 project- Now what?||||1||
Z||000000||Cooter||05-21-2000||11:39 PM||||I spent most of today in the garage assembling the bolt-ons to the engine. Got ready to put on the oil pan and it doesnt fit. The 3.4 has a different pickup tube than the 2.8 and the pans are different. The pickup tubes are different diameters, so they won't interchange. Now what? Order a high volume pump or stock replacement for the 2.8 or use the 3.4's oil pan and have to hack up a front motor mount to make it fit over the pan? This is getting on my nerves!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Alex4mula||05-22-2000||08:59 AM||||I'm a little ahead of you on my 3.4 project. What year car is yours? I have Formula (88). I bought my 3.4 from Ed Parks. The new oil pump and tube were exactly the same as my 2.8. So I used my oil pan without problems. I know there are some matches to do on pre 88s regarding oil pans and timing covers but don't know exactly. Check Ed's page for that. What is the condition of your old pump? You may use it or just buy a replacement. Also in my case Ed provided a front mount bracket that did not work. I think it was wleded incorrectly. I was able to use mine by just cutting one tab where a screw hole is near the A/C bracket and grinding a little. Other than that everything fits OK. The oil dip stick hole in the block is also a little bit larger. I was able to find an O-ring to put in the tube that will seal it good. If you need the size let me know.||||reg||1
Z||000002||86SE||08-22-2000||02:29 AM||||So what ever happened Cooter?  What year is your Fiero?  I am trying to get all the parts for my near future pushrod 3.4 and need to know all the parts to get.  <P>Do I need a new pump?  If so, what kind?<P>Do I need a new pan?  I think my 3.4 will come with a pan (93-95 Camaro), so do I use my Fiero pan?<P>Or can I just mod my front motor mount to make it fit?<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||5
Z||000003||88formula||08-22-2000||08:03 PM||||Use the fiero pan and cover. Use all the fiero 2.8 parts and don't forget the check valve in the oil filter passage that you must transfer over from the 2.8 to the 3.4 engine. Use the fiero front cover and water pump also. <P>As for cooter, I thought you already had your 3.4 in and running. You sent me a video of you in your 3.4 fiero right? You have to cut a slot in the 2.8 pan so that the 3.4 oil pump will fit I think. Check this link out if you don't already have it.  <A HREF="http://members.home.net/sprigint/x11p/x11p.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.home.net/sprigint/x11p/x11p.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||08-22-2000||09:17 PM||||Wow! A thread from the past. I have about 500 miles on the 3.4. It is in an 88GT with the "performance" 4.10 geared four speed that came from Spyder(Thanks!)<P>I used all the external stuff from the 2.8. <BR>Timing cover, oil pan, valve covers, complete intake system, distributer etc... The oil filter mounting is different too- that check valve comes out of the 2.8 block pretty easy. You can see it with the filter off pretty easy. The oil pressure guage lines are different sizes from the 2.8 to the 3.4- I found some brass fittings at Lowe's that made it all fit together again. THere are some temp. sensors in the heads that will have to be swapped into the 3.4 also. I believe that the 3.4 has a pipe plug in the head where the Fiero has a sensor. I had to hammer and chisal the pipe plug out of the 3.4 head and retap the hole. It has locktite on it and nothing I had would fit the square head hole in the plug.<P>My oil pan baffle prevented the use of the 3.4's oil pump because the pickup is different. I tried swapping the pickups, but the tube is a different size. Because the 3.4 had 70,000 on it I just bought a replacement pump for a Fiero 2.8 and installed it. Not a high volume, just a regular "cheap" pump and it holds well over 60 PSI at running speeds.<P>BTW- The car really performs well- better than I expected. But I also have a better exhaust system with the larger passage Camaro manifolds, gasket matched intake components, bored out throttle body, extremly thin throttle butterfly, MSD 6a and blaster coil and free flowing hood scoop air intake. <P>------------------<BR>The slower they think you are, the more suprised they will be when you smoke them.||||reg||1
